        "content_text": "<a href=\"https://www.seattletimes.com/pacific-nw-magazine/how-snoqualmie-pass-became-the-most-important-route-across-the-cascades/\">For one perspective on peopling the passes, and one that showcases the transition from two-legged to wheeled travel, consider Snoqualmie Pass, where Interstate 90 cuts through the Cascades. People began using this lowest route &#8212; 3,004 feet &#8212; over the mountains around 9,000 years ago.</a> (Seattle Times, <a href=\"https://archive.is/5in3T\">archive</a>)",
